<!--Post: 3718--><!--Time: 1105159362-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3718"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Iceteajan<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Iceteajan<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, January 07, 2005 - 8:42 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Wasn’t there was one BTK fingerprint left behind on a chair at one of the crime scenes? <BR>   <BR>Did BTK have some military background? <BR> <BR>Did the military customarily take/archive fingerprints on enlisted personnel? <BR> <BR>Okay, you see where this is going!! <BR> <BR>Would fingerprint comparisons be such an overwhelming task in this electronic scanning age? <BR> <BR>Even though one would think that performing such a search would be preliminary/rudimentary, do we really know if they have indeed performed such a search recently &#40;now that we are in the electronic age of 2005&#41;?   <BR> <BR>I realize we would need to absolutely sort the list down: <BR> <BR>--Enlisted personnel that resided in Wichita, went to a High School/College in Wichita &#40;this information should be SOMEWHERE in the military records!&#41; <BR> <BR>--Gender:  Male <BR> <BR>--Approximate Age &amp; Approximate time of enlistment <BR> <BR>--Hispanic? <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3718-->
<!--Post: 3843--><!--Time: 1105322572-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3843"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Chiefron<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Chiefron<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 09, 2005 - 6:02 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->This is one of the strongest reasons to believe that BTK was lying about military service. At the time that BTK says he servered the military had been fingerprinting new recruits at the Military Entrance Processing centers for years. Those fingerprint cards were kept both in the members record and on file in D.C. at the Bureau of Personel &#40;now in Millington TN along with DNA samples since the mid 90s&#41;, for identification purposes.  <BR> <BR>It is possible that the records were destroyed for space purposes or lost/destroyed during the conversion of stored records to microfiche in the 70s.  <BR> <BR>Everything you listed would exist in many places in the persons record, most of it on his enlistment contract. In fact it contains a lot of other stuff as well including police record if any, known aliases, list or residences over previous 7 years,etc.  <BR> <BR>If the records still exist however, they are old enough that I doubt they have been digitized. The prints would not be in NCIC either. So any search would be an exhaustive one. First you would have to identify all possible suspects from the time, pull the records, print them from film, scan them into the system and begin comparisons. Even then there is no way to be fully certain BTK enlisted from the Wichita area, or that he was ever in the military. I even suspect that he may have tailored his &#34;biography&#34; to police along the lines of their own profile of him. Although I have a lot of respect for FBI profilers, some bits of his life story mesh a bit too well with the profile. <BR> <BR>Ron<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3843-->
<!--Post: 3892--><!--Time: 1105402272-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3892"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->X3maine<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->X3maine<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, January 10, 2005 - 4:11 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->remember BTK&#39;s mention of Petra and Tina in his &#34;bio&#34;...  and then think about the graphic Tom put up on the site of &#34;Pet rat in a...&#34;  &#40;petra &#43; tina = pet rat in a&#41; <BR> <BR>Buddy The Koala is sending everyone on a wild goose chase with the info he let out. He does not want to be caught. <BR> <BR>However, IF the old military records are ever converted to digital format, then it would definitely be a worthwhile check.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3892-->
<!--Post: 4201--><!--Time: 1105849771-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4201"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Angrywolf<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Angrywolf<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 15, 2005 - 8:29 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I wouldn&#39;t be so sure that fingerprints existed...or would still exist...BTK could have been in a foreign military service and immigrated to the US..so his fingerprints would not be on file..Even if they were..look at all the trouble they had finding Bush&#39;s records and you&#39;ll see they may have been lost or destroyed...<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4201-->
<!--Post: 4295--><!--Time: 1106064042-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4295"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Chiefron<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Chiefron<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 18, 2005 - 8:00 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Angry, <BR> <BR>If he immigrated to the United States his fingerprints would most assuredly be on record. My wife and 2 oldest children are foreign citizens who hold permanent residency here. When we moved back to the United States it was a 3 year process to immigate them &#40;though they were allowed to reside with me during that time&#41;. There are several steps to the process, but at each step they were printed and photographed. In the old days those prints would have been both in his passport and later on his &#34;green&#34; card. The same goes today, except the green card is no longer green &#40;its a peach color in case anyone wanted to know&#41;. Copies of the original print cards are held by the Naturalization and Immigration Service. One of the biggest contributors to the NCICs digital fingerprint database when it was created was the NIS and EPIC. If he served in a foreign military that would have been of definite interest, especially at the time and the INS would probably have requested a report from EPIC.  <BR> <BR>Bush&#39;s records a long with a few thousand other Guard personnel files from a specific storage facility were lost due in part to a fire at that facility during the period that they were being transfered from paper to microfilm. His case is the exception, not the rule. I was able to pull my father&#39;s entire Navy personnel file and he served 20 years before Bush.  <BR> <BR>Ron<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4295-->
<!--Post: 4297--><!--Time: 1106068798-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4297"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Iceteajan<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Iceteajan<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, January 18, 2005 - 9:19 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Does anyone know if any fingerprints &#40;full or partial&#41; were lifted from the phone receiver &#40;or key pad&#41; that BTK used to make his notorious phone call?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4297-->
<!--Post: 7503--><!--Time: 1108527780-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7503"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:williammichael@direcway.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Oklahoma_mike<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Oklahoma_mike<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 15, 2005 - 8:23 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I am not sure if the payphone whould have a keypunch or a dial back in 1977, so there may be no keys to dust for prints!  Any local WIchitans know when payphones switched type?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7503-->
<!--Post: 7851--><!--Time: 1108710905-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7851"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Stormy74<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Stormy74<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, February 17, 2005 - 11:15 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->That is a great question. Don&#39;t they have photos or some kind of footage from back then that maybe we could tell if it was rotary or punch? It would be awesome if they would have thought of that!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7851-->
<!--Post: 7964--><!--Time: 1108768143-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7964"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Johno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Johno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 18, 2005 - 3:09 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Iceteajan, back to your initial post on a fingerprint on a chair at one of the crime scenes I think that was found at the Otero crime scene. <BR>Remember they had recently within two months had moved into that house.  <BR>Perhaps the fingerprint could have been from one of the moving company men and since no one else touched the chair in that particular spot since then the print remained intact. <BR>If there was a moving company that moved them I&#39;m sure investigators would have checked out who the movers were. A mover would have been a good way to get to know the Otero house and the Otero&#39;s. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7964-->
<!--Post: 11222--><!--Time: 1110508230-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11222"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Haytrader<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Haytrader<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 10, 2005 - 6:30 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I had an appointment at the V A in Wichita today. I asked a nurse I had prior talks with about BTK if he had been there. She said NO, they had checked. She said they were both surprised but relieved. Not all veterans use the V A services. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11222-->
